"We want to be engaged.

00:20:24.445 --> 00:20:27.011
"We want to be passionate
about this thing."

00:20:27.011 --> 00:20:28.392
Yeah.
And also,

00:20:28.392 --> 00:20:31.142
we're really, really, really busy

00:20:32.873 --> 00:20:37.206
and we can devote about a
day to shooting an episode

00:20:38.772 --> 00:20:39.605
just FYI.

00:20:41.558 --> 00:20:42.553
You know what I'm saying?
Yeah.

00:20:42.553 --> 00:20:45.851
And they wasn't like,
oh no we can't do that.

00:20:45.851 --> 00:20:47.378
It was like, well okay actually

00:20:47.378 --> 00:20:50.200
that's what we were thinking
too because you know,

00:20:50.200 --> 00:20:53.537
with all the stuff that's being
made in the world right now,

00:20:53.537 --> 00:20:57.572
they're not just throwing
out massive amounts

00:20:57.572 --> 00:21:00.080
of money for the budgets
on these shows right?

00:21:00.080 --> 00:21:02.573
It's like, people are trying to make shows

00:21:02.573 --> 00:21:04.689
much more efficient than they used to.

00:21:04.689 --> 00:21:06.815
So being able to produce
something in a day

00:21:06.815 --> 00:21:08.860
or shoot something a
day is actually the page

00:21:08.860 --> 00:21:10.210
that everybody was already on.

00:21:10.210 --> 00:21:12.305
So then we started asking
the question of like,

00:21:12.305 --> 00:21:15.430
all right, this loose concept of,

00:21:15.430 --> 00:21:17.515
it totally makes sense why
they would approach us.

00:21:17.515 --> 00:21:19.104
We've tried all this food.

00:21:19.104 --> 00:21:20.754
We've eaten all these snacks.

00:21:20.754 --> 00:21:24.203
We've kind of positioned
ourselves as your buddies

00:21:24.203 --> 00:21:27.439
behind the desk who eat
all the stuff for you

00:21:27.439 --> 00:21:29.005
to tell you if you should eat it.

00:21:29.005 --> 00:21:31.478
Yeah people who taste
things and talk about it.

00:21:31.478 --> 00:21:32.983
Right, not chefs.

00:21:32.983 --> 00:21:35.887
If I didn't have to clarify that.

00:21:35.887 --> 00:21:39.061
I didn't think I had to clarify that, yes.

00:21:39.061 --> 00:21:41.394
Not chefs but food-adjacent,

00:21:43.477 --> 00:21:45.742
or actually directly related to food

00:21:45.742 --> 00:21:46.794
but not guys who make it.

00:21:46.794 --> 00:21:47.650
Just guys who eat it.

00:21:47.650 --> 00:21:48.606
Just like you.

00:21:48.606 --> 00:21:50.094
Food friends.

00:21:50.094 --> 00:21:53.569
And then the process
started it was like well.

00:21:53.569 --> 00:21:56.178
Could've called it that, Food Friends.

00:21:56.178 --> 00:21:57.744
Well we got close to calling it something.

00:21:57.744 --> 00:21:59.516
That's true.
It was called something else

00:21:59.516 --> 00:22:00.349
at one point.

00:22:00.349 --> 00:22:01.327
We can get into that a little bit.

00:22:01.327 --> 00:22:02.677
There was a couple of things happening

00:22:02.677 --> 00:22:06.160
sort of behind the scenes
and so there was a desire

00:22:06.160 --> 00:22:10.853
to differentiate this from just
kind of going into a factory

00:22:10.853 --> 00:22:12.956
and just dealing with snacks.

00:22:12.956 --> 00:22:13.789
It was like,

00:22:13.789 --> 00:22:17.606
can we expand that into just
America's favorite food brands?

00:22:17.606 --> 00:22:19.238
So that might be a restaurant.

00:22:19.238 --> 00:22:21.968
It might be somebody
who makes this a snack

00:22:21.968 --> 00:22:25.468
but just a food or a place that makes food

00:22:27.187 --> 00:22:29.696
that everyone kind of knows and relates to

00:22:29.696 --> 00:22:31.507
and has some sort of
point of reference to.

00:22:31.507 --> 00:22:34.147
Yeah, which is kind of our mentality

00:22:34.147 --> 00:22:35.378
on Good Mythical Morning.

00:22:35.378 --> 00:22:40.295
The more we can pull from and
experience well-known brands,

00:22:41.968 --> 00:22:44.947
whether that's fast food brands.

00:22:44.947 --> 00:22:47.080
You know, sit down restaurant brands

00:22:47.080 --> 00:22:50.122
or different types of
potato chips or cereals.

00:22:50.122 --> 00:22:52.553
That are you know, stuff you
can find in the grocery store

00:22:52.553 --> 00:22:54.038
or on the snack aisle.

00:22:54.038 --> 00:22:58.013
Like there's that connection
that there's people

00:22:58.013 --> 00:23:00.129
that are passionate about certain things

00:23:00.129 --> 00:23:01.520
and we're very passionate
about certain things.

00:23:01.520 --> 00:23:03.820
So tapping into that
passion and then saying,

00:23:03.820 --> 00:23:06.385
"Okay, well let's peel back the curtain

00:23:06.385 --> 00:23:08.586
"and show you behind the scenes there."

00:23:08.586 --> 00:23:09.742
But there's so much more

00:23:09.742 --> 00:23:11.514
than just that whole factory thing anyway.

00:23:11.514 --> 00:23:14.513
That like, this show can be more expansive

00:23:14.513 --> 00:23:16.629
and then it got more exciting.

00:23:16.629 --> 00:23:20.376
And then we had the idea to like,

00:23:20.376 --> 00:23:23.945
how do we sort of Rhett and
Linkize this thing in terms of

00:23:23.945 --> 00:23:28.088
we knew that we wanted it to
be very comedy-forward right?

00:23:28.088 --> 00:23:30.230
And we also knew that we wanted it to be

00:23:30.230 --> 00:23:32.020
very curiosity-forward

00:23:32.020 --> 00:23:34.251
because those are kind of
the two pillars of our brand.

00:23:34.251 --> 00:23:35.820
Curiosity and comedy.

00:23:35.820 --> 00:23:40.065
And so that was when we
established this idea

00:23:40.065 --> 00:23:42.618
of what if there was
sort of an off the wall,

00:23:42.618 --> 00:23:47.618
sort of out of left field
question that we want to ask

00:23:47.782 --> 00:23:49.366
about this particular brand.

00:23:49.366 --> 00:23:53.208
Like when we think Chipotle,
this is what we think.

00:23:53.208 --> 00:23:55.363
When we think about Cheesecake Factory,

00:23:55.363 --> 00:23:57.518
this is the question
that comes to mind for us

00:23:57.518 --> 00:24:00.136
and then can we craft an
episode around that question

00:24:00.136 --> 00:24:01.873
and kind of getting,

00:24:01.873 --> 00:24:04.073
going behind the scenes
to answer that question.

00:24:04.073 --> 00:24:05.701
Before Chipotle or Cheesecake Factory

00:24:05.701 --> 00:24:08.009
became actual episodes,

00:24:08.009 --> 00:24:11.095
do you remember the first
example we talked about?

00:24:11.095 --> 00:24:14.725
I do remember 'cause I came
up with it in a phone call.

00:24:14.725 --> 00:24:16.628
Yeah it was like you know,

00:24:16.628 --> 00:24:20.215
we were having these
working meetings with B17.

00:24:20.215 --> 00:24:21.048
Me, you,

00:24:22.269 --> 00:24:25.728
Stevie and our development
executive at the time, Mallory.

00:24:25.728 --> 00:24:27.928
We were kind of like shaping it.

00:24:27.928 --> 00:24:31.010
It was fun and so we were coming up with,

00:24:31.010 --> 00:24:33.074
I remember it was a very
preliminary conversation.

00:24:33.074 --> 00:24:36.053
We were talking about
this line of questioning,

00:24:36.053 --> 00:24:37.586
being inquisitive.
Right.

00:24:37.586 --> 00:24:40.669
And could one question become like,

00:24:42.838 --> 00:24:45.043
a starting point for an episode

00:24:45.043 --> 00:24:47.238
or maybe a throughline for an episode

00:24:47.238 --> 00:24:49.242
but when you're brainstorming,

00:24:49.242 --> 00:24:52.804
when you speak in abstract terms,

00:24:52.804 --> 00:24:55.411
you can only get so excited.

00:24:55.411 --> 00:24:57.661
And so if in those moments,

00:24:58.821 --> 00:25:03.821
on those like nascent calls we
can throw something out there

00:25:04.463 --> 00:25:06.928
that you can rally around,

00:25:06.928 --> 00:25:09.083
it makes all the difference in the world.

00:25:09.083 --> 00:25:12.833
And so that's when you
threw out the example.

00:25:14.725 --> 00:25:16.893
I don't remember how I
phrased the question.

00:25:16.893 --> 00:25:17.726
I thought you said you remembered it.

00:25:17.726 --> 00:25:18.845
No I know the question.

00:25:18.845 --> 00:25:21.127
The question was about the foldy chip.

00:25:21.127 --> 00:25:25.460
Yeah like say we're going
to a potato chip factory

00:25:27.280 --> 00:25:30.084
to look at like, we're a big fan of Lay's.

00:25:30.084 --> 00:25:32.669
Let's see if we could go to
a Lay's potato chip factory.

00:25:32.669 --> 00:25:34.268
And I think the question was essentially,

00:25:34.268 --> 00:25:36.207
how did the foldy chips happen

00:25:36.207 --> 00:25:39.752
and how does this brand
feel about the foldy chips?

00:25:39.752 --> 00:25:42.906
And that came from the fact
that you have talked about

00:25:42.906 --> 00:25:44.186
how you like the foldy chips.

00:25:44.186 --> 00:25:45.377
You prefer the foldy chips.

00:25:45.377 --> 00:25:46.917
You go after the foldy chips.

00:25:46.917 --> 00:25:49.740
I've even said I want a bag
of nothing but foldy chips.

00:25:49.740 --> 00:25:50.649
And so I think.
And I ain't talking

00:25:50.649 --> 00:25:51.800
about kettle cut,

00:25:51.800 --> 00:25:53.981
Kettle chips.
And I think the question.

00:25:53.981 --> 00:25:57.536
What I had in mind was is
the foldy chip a mistake

00:25:57.536 --> 00:25:59.952
or is the foldy chip a beautiful mistake

00:25:59.952 --> 00:26:01.412
or is the foldy chip?

00:26:01.412 --> 00:26:03.495
Is it?
An accepted error

00:26:04.495 --> 00:26:06.828
or is it part of the design?

00:26:07.846 --> 00:26:09.792
Right.
And how,

00:26:09.792 --> 00:26:12.042
yeah how do the executives?

00:26:13.001 --> 00:26:16.631
How do the executive chefs
at the potato chip factory

00:26:16.631 --> 00:26:18.762
or like the quality control people?

00:26:18.762 --> 00:26:22.302
You know, we started
salivating over these answers.

00:26:22.302 --> 00:26:25.374
Right.
And everybody on the call

00:26:25.374 --> 00:26:27.341
got excited about it.

00:26:27.341 --> 00:26:30.085
So that became the clarion call.

00:26:30.085 --> 00:26:31.912
Yep.
Of the curiosity

00:26:31.912 --> 00:26:35.448
associated with Inside Eats even though

00:26:35.448 --> 00:26:36.607
we weren't calling it that yet.

00:26:36.607 --> 00:26:39.740
And then of course, as
this was developing.

00:26:39.740 --> 00:26:44.537
Now first of all, everybody
was like yes, this is great.

00:26:44.537 --> 00:26:46.629
Now the process of,

00:26:46.629 --> 00:26:48.973
I mean you gotta be making phone calls to,

00:26:48.973 --> 00:26:51.419
B17 is reaching out to all these brands.

00:26:51.419 --> 00:26:52.828
Because you gotta be like,

00:26:52.828 --> 00:26:54.102
Hey these two guys.

00:26:54.102 --> 00:26:55.852
Here you can go to their YouTube,

00:26:55.852 --> 00:26:56.685
you can see who they are.

00:26:56.685 --> 00:26:59.217
They're going to come to your place

00:26:59.217 --> 00:27:02.051
and they're going to sort
of go behind the scenes

00:27:02.051 --> 00:27:05.818
for this Food Network Show and I mean,

00:27:05.818 --> 00:27:09.866
not everybody is comfortable
with us coming into their,

00:27:09.866 --> 00:27:13.711
finding out about what they're
doing but in some ways.

00:27:13.711 --> 00:27:17.026
I would say most brands
were excited about it

00:27:17.026 --> 00:27:19.241
because I mean, let's just face it.

00:27:19.241 --> 00:27:20.883
You're gonna be, after
you watch an episode,

00:27:20.883 --> 00:27:23.379
you're gonna be hungry for
whatever this thing is.

00:27:23.379 --> 00:27:25.432
But I mean you have to sell that

00:27:25.432 --> 00:27:27.879
'cause the show doesn't
exist and you know,

00:27:27.879 --> 00:27:30.034
there is a level of trust that a brand

00:27:30.034 --> 00:27:32.837
has to put on the line
and it's so easy for,

00:27:32.837 --> 00:27:36.994
especially when you go to
like corporatized brands.

00:27:36.994 --> 00:27:39.601
Like someone as big as
Chipotle could easily have,

00:27:39.601 --> 00:27:42.150
somebody's job's on the line.

00:27:42.150 --> 00:27:45.767
And they could just want to
cover their own ass and say,

00:27:45.767 --> 00:27:48.819
"Well there's just as much
to lose as there is to gain.

00:27:48.819 --> 00:27:51.173
"What if these guys embarrass us?

00:27:51.173 --> 00:27:53.156
"You know, I don't know
what exactly this show is.

00:27:53.156 --> 00:27:55.885
"But it is Food Network and I do,

00:27:55.885 --> 00:27:58.208
"I can see other things
these guys have done."

00:27:58.208 --> 00:27:59.136
And you know,

00:27:59.136 --> 00:28:00.191
B17 did a good job.
We're respect.

00:28:00.191 --> 00:28:01.024
We're good boys.

00:28:01.024 --> 00:28:02.032
We're good boys.
We're good boys.

00:28:02.032 --> 00:28:03.336
We're not going to embarrass you too much.

00:28:03.336 --> 00:28:06.919
I mean we've talked some food on our show

00:28:07.948 --> 00:28:09.694
just because hey, you gotta be honest

00:28:09.694 --> 00:28:10.856
about what you like.
You gotta stay honest.

00:28:10.856 --> 00:28:14.856
But yeah luckily some
very high profile brands

00:28:16.438 --> 00:28:19.384
like Chipotle's huge you know?

00:28:19.384 --> 00:28:22.016
To see, to get the vision for it and say,

00:28:22.016 --> 00:28:26.110
"Hey, this could be great for
us and everybody could win."

00:28:26.110 --> 00:28:27.813
And we had to find four brands,

00:28:27.813 --> 00:28:30.122
just to give you a little
background on this.

00:28:30.122 --> 00:28:31.100
So.
Right.

00:28:31.100 --> 00:28:34.139
Season one of Inside
Eats is four episodes.

00:28:34.139 --> 00:28:35.566
I mean four episodes, that's a short.

00:28:35.566 --> 00:28:38.154
That's just, that's how Discovery

00:28:38.154 --> 00:28:40.340
or Food Network is doing things right now.

00:28:40.340 --> 00:28:42.864
It's like first episode, first season,

00:28:42.864 --> 00:28:45.064
let's do four episodes,
see what people think.

00:28:45.064 --> 00:28:46.242
At least they didn't order a pilot

00:28:46.242 --> 00:28:49.992
and then maybe we work on
that and then no one sees it.

00:28:49.992 --> 00:28:50.825
Yeah.
If they don't like it,

00:28:50.825 --> 00:28:53.266
it's like, they're gonna make an order.

00:28:53.266 --> 00:28:56.056
I feel like it's kind of an extended pilot

00:28:56.056 --> 00:28:59.061
'cause four is a short season.

00:28:59.061 --> 00:29:00.285
But I think it's,

00:29:00.285 --> 00:29:02.458
you really learn a lot
as we'll talk about.

00:29:02.458 --> 00:29:04.155
Yeah.
That then you can

00:29:04.155 --> 00:29:08.423
really take it, take all that into account

00:29:08.423 --> 00:29:10.173
when they order more.

00:29:11.789 --> 00:29:13.056
And I certainly hope they do.

00:29:13.056 --> 00:29:14.034
Yeah, well 'cause we learned.

00:29:14.034 --> 00:29:15.919
We'll get into how much we've learned

00:29:15.919 --> 00:29:17.615
in just doing the first four but,

00:29:17.615 --> 00:29:20.282
so the brands that responded.

00:29:20.282 --> 00:29:24.372
Actually more than a
handful of brands responded

00:29:24.372 --> 00:29:25.550
and said they were into the idea

00:29:25.550 --> 00:29:28.529
and the four that ended up
working out based on timing.

00:29:28.529 --> 00:29:29.924
Because this all had to happen.

00:29:29.924 --> 00:29:31.766
This moved very quickly

00:29:31.766 --> 00:29:34.031
and it had to happen at the end of 2021.

00:29:34.031 --> 00:29:38.170
We shot all four episodes over
the course of about two weeks

00:29:38.170 --> 00:29:40.960
in literally, like I said,
four actual days of shooting.

00:29:40.960 --> 00:29:43.128
Like two days in a row and
then like two weeks later

00:29:43.128 --> 00:29:43.961
two more days.

00:29:43.961 --> 00:29:46.461
Right before Christmas.

00:29:46.461 --> 00:29:50.151
And the companies that we ended
up going with were Chipotle,

00:29:50.151 --> 00:29:52.918
Cheesecake Factory, Coolhaus ice cream

00:29:52.918 --> 00:29:55.095
which is a very cool ice cream shop.

00:29:55.095 --> 00:29:57.309
That actually is available nationwide.

00:29:57.309 --> 00:29:59.804
You probably have seen
them at the grocery store

00:29:59.804 --> 00:30:00.720
but they're based in L.A..
Their pint's

00:30:00.720 --> 00:30:02.099
next to like Ben &amp; Jerry's.

00:30:02.099 --> 00:30:04.317
And then Beyond Meat who we've
talked about on the show.

00:30:04.317 --> 00:30:08.267
You know basically making
plant-based meat products

00:30:08.267 --> 00:30:11.291
and they had never had anybody
enter behind the scenes.

00:30:11.291 --> 00:30:12.470
But anyway,

00:30:12.470 --> 00:30:15.925
those are the four that
are featured in season one.

00:30:15.925 --> 00:30:17.648
And for the first episode,

00:30:17.648 --> 00:30:21.584
since that's the one that
you can watch right now.

00:30:21.584 --> 00:30:23.182
The thing that we talked about

00:30:23.182 --> 00:30:27.334
'cause this is something that
we've sort of talked about

00:30:27.334 --> 00:30:30.293
tangentially before is this like,

00:30:30.293 --> 00:30:32.602
when you go to Chipotle.

00:30:32.602 --> 00:30:33.435
Are you a burrito guy?

00:30:33.435 --> 00:30:34.636
Are you a bowl girl?

00:30:34.636 --> 00:30:36.719
Are you a quesadilla kid?

00:30:37.951 --> 00:30:39.036
Whatever, I'm not saying

00:30:39.036 --> 00:30:40.696
that that's who gets those things.

00:30:40.696 --> 00:30:41.734
I'm just you.
Are you a salad robot?

00:30:41.734 --> 00:30:45.151
Yeah and we wanted to ask the question,

00:30:47.466 --> 00:30:51.236
does what you order at
Chipotle say something

00:30:51.236 --> 00:30:54.273
about your personality and
maybe more specifically,

00:30:54.273 --> 00:30:56.106
does your personality,

00:30:57.157 --> 00:30:58.827
can we know things about your personality

00:30:58.827 --> 00:31:02.596
and then predict what you're
going to get at Chipotle?

00:31:02.596 --> 00:31:05.947
Yeah and at least, in
the least we thought,

00:31:05.947 --> 00:31:08.656
"Well it'll be fun to try."

00:31:08.656 --> 00:31:10.145
Yes.
And it was.

00:31:10.145 --> 00:31:11.927
So very much in the same way

00:31:11.927 --> 00:31:13.920
that we approach things on GMM where

00:31:13.920 --> 00:31:18.824
we delve into research and science

00:31:18.824 --> 00:31:23.012
but we do it from a comedic
standpoint, that is,

00:31:23.012 --> 00:31:25.986
that's really how we approach the show.

00:31:25.986 --> 00:31:26.962
Especially that first episode

00:31:26.962 --> 00:31:29.103
'cause it was kind of a
social science question.

00:31:29.103 --> 00:31:30.467
Yeah for Cheesecake Factory,

00:31:30.467 --> 00:31:33.392
we knew we had to make the episode

00:31:33.392 --> 00:31:36.783
ultimately about the
bigness of their menu.

00:31:36.783 --> 00:31:39.221
How could their menu be so large?

00:31:39.221 --> 00:31:40.054
Like why?

00:31:41.064 --> 00:31:42.587
But also how?

00:31:42.587 --> 00:31:43.549
If you think about it.

00:31:43.549 --> 00:31:48.012
It's a crazy amount of dishes
to come out of one kitchen

00:31:48.012 --> 00:31:51.711
that then is replicated in
all their different locations.

00:31:51.711 --> 00:31:53.128
It's pretty wild.

00:31:54.446 --> 00:31:57.779
And then for Coolhaus, because they had.

00:32:00.011 --> 00:32:04.473
I mean arguably pushed the
flavors of ice cream just as far,

00:32:04.473 --> 00:32:07.901
if not farther than any
other ice cream maker,

00:32:07.901 --> 00:32:10.824
I mean that inspired us to say,

00:32:10.824 --> 00:32:12.733
"Well how far is too far?"

00:32:12.733 --> 00:32:17.331
And would they let us
